ust checked (action 0), meaning they didn't raise and simply called the big blind.\n\nLet me consider several options:\n\n1. **Check (0)**: This is very passive with such a strong hand. Pocket 10s are well above average and should be played aggressively to build the pot and protect against overcards on the flop.\n\n2. **Fold (-1)**: Absolutely not an option with pocket 10s, one of the better starting hands in poker.\n\n3. **Raise (200+)**: This is the standard play. A raise of 200-400 chips would be a solid 2-4x big blind raise (assuming standard blind structure). This builds the pot when I likely have the best hand and puts pressure on my opponent.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Too aggressive for this stage. While pocket 10s is strong, going all-in pre-flop in a cash game scenario is only warranted with premium hands like AA, KK, or sometimes QQ. This would also be poor strategy as it doesn't allow me to extract maximum value.\n\n**Strategic considerations**:\n- I'm currently down 350 chips cumulatively and need to start winning to catch up\n- Pocket 10s is approximately the 5th best starting hand in heads-up play\n- My opponent checked, showing weakness or a trapping play\n- A standard raise allows me to build the pot while maintaining flexibility\n- With 15 hands played and 5 remaining, I need to capitalize on strong hands\n\nReason: \nPocket 10s is a premium starting hand that warrants aggressive play. My opponent's check indicates weakness or a marginal hand. By raising to 200 (a standard 2x big blind raise), I can build the pot with my strong hand, gain information about my opponent's holding, and maintain control of the hand. This balanced approach maximizes value while minimizing risk compared to checking or going all-in.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
